 "name": "S8: Ch4, Team structures",
 "text_content": "Pages 261 to 281. Chapter 4, Team structures.\n\nS\u014fn output (draft): org design.\n\nChef-gated, in part. BOH structure is the chef partner's to shape from the beginning. That is a stated structural position, not a gap. Build the FOH org and the shared spine. Mark the BOH structure explicitly chef-gated and hold it for early in that hire's onboarding. Do not stall the session on it.\n\nAdaptation required: her team structures assume one org with one operating register. S\u014fn runs four dayparts under one roof, with a stated thesis that dayparts compound rather than reset. That is a different org problem. It is closer to four shifts of one team than four teams.",
 "description": "Pages 261 to 281. Chapter 4, Team structures.\n\nS\u014fn output (draft): org design.\n\nChef-gated, in part. BOH structure is the chef partner's to shape from the beginning. That is a stated structural position, not a gap. Build the FOH org and the shared spine. Mark the BOH structure explicitly chef-gated and hold it for early in that hire's onboarding. Do not stall the session on it.\n\nAdaptation required: her team structures assume one org with one operating register. S\u014fn runs four dayparts under one roof, with a stated thesis that dayparts compound rather than reset. That is a different org problem. It is closer to four shifts of one team than four teams.",
 "markdown_description": "**Pages 261 to 281.** Chapter 4, Team structures.\n\n**S\u014fn output (draft):** org design.\n\n**Chef-gated, in part.** BOH structure is the chef partner's to shape from the beginning. That is a stated structural position, not a gap. Build the FOH org and the shared spine. Mark the BOH structure explicitly chef-gated and hold it for early in that hire's onboarding. Do not stall the session on it.\n\n**Adaptation required:** her team structures assume one org with one operating register. S\u014fn runs four dayparts under one roof, with a stated thesis that dayparts compound rather than reset. That is a different org problem. It is closer to four shifts of one team than four teams.",
